out ways to sustain the hype and interest in these opportunities.

191
00:19:29,760 --> 00:19:32,160
Why do you think people like Athena so much?

192
00:19:32,160 --> 00:19:37,200
I think they're not quite the first mover, but I want to say they're definitely one of the most

193
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:42,640
competent teams that we've worked with. Guy and his team, they have a very clear idea of where

194
00:19:42,640 --> 00:19:50,000
they want to be, and their execution is actually very, very strong. And I think with stable coins,

195
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:56,960
the playbook is typically more bd-heavy than tech-heavy. So the partnership

196
00:19:56,960 --> 00:20:02,960
lineups that they have is very impressive. And then to be able to have markets set up on

197
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:08,000
Bybit and a couple of other exchanges. So basically having USD pair, I think that helps

198
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:12,640
tremendously as well. So figuring out ways to get their asset distributed,

199
00:20:13,200 --> 00:20:19,760
I think that has been a very, very strong proposition of Athena. And I can see them

200
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:24,800
growing even more from here. Yeah. I don't think you can underscore the value of those partnerships.

201
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:29,040
We're naturally very herd mentality. People who deposit in DeFi, no one wants to deposit

202
00:20:29,040 --> 00:20:33,840
into the stable coin with $500,000 of supply because you just don't have the trust factor yet.

203
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:42,800
Yes. In your view, is it the same user who was earning Apecoin yield a couple of years ago that

204
00:20:42,800 --> 00:20:48,480
is earning Athena or earning level USD yield now, or is it a different user profile?

205
00:20:48,480 --> 00:20:55,040
I think the profile has not changed all that much. I think people who are speculating on YTs,

206
00:20:55,040 --> 00:21:01,440
at least from our observation, they are largely retail users because it's very capital efficient.

207
00:21:01,440 --> 00:21:08,320
If you want to speculate on the potential return of say, Athena points, there's a good amount of

208
00:21:08,320 --> 00:21:14,320
multiplier to offer that margin of safety. PTs on the other hand, we see a lot more bigger funds,

209
00:21:14,320 --> 00:21:19,840
bigger ticket size participating in that segment of the market. I think for us, even though the

210
00:21:19,840 --> 00:21:26,080
product is fairly complicated, especially if you don't really understand derivative, I think

211
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:31,440
understanding Pendle can take a little bit of time. With that knowledge, we were still quite

212
00:21:31,440 --> 00:21:37,440
insistent on targeting the sophisticated retail first, more so than targeting the institutional

213
00:21:37,440 --> 00:21:43,680
users. This is something that we learned over time and it applies to how we go to market for

214
00:21:43,680 --> 00:21:49,680
various stable coins or even something like Boros. With respect to product like ours,

215
00:21:50,400 --> 00:21:56,400
especially because it involves some form of speculation, the target user group that we

216
00:21:56,400 --> 00:22:01,760
should be focusing on in the beginning are likely the sophisticated retail. They are the ones that

217
00:22:01,760 --> 00:22:07,120
typically have higher risk tolerance and then they would be more willing to try out new products and

218
00:22:07,120 --> 00:22:13,520
they would be very interested in that outside's earning. We just have to focus on this core group

219
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:20,640
of users. Then we realized that over time, when the traction of this segment improved,

220
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:26,080
because these are probably users who are punting on YT's, it also creates a very good opportunity

221
00:22:26,080 --> 00:22:34,160
on the PT side. When PT becomes cheaper, then the big funds have a reason to come in and scoop up

222
00:22:34,160 --> 00:22:39,440
like the PT's. That's how we established that dynamic and the equilibrium between the YT and

223
00:22:39,440 --> 00:22:47,680
PT side. Do you think that DeFi yields outpace or remain competitive to traditional yields,

224
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:54,000
especially as on-chain and traditional systems blend together? Yes, I do believe that to be the

225
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:59,520
case. Broadly speaking, how I think about the yield at DeFi is that it will very likely be

226
00:22:59,520 --> 00:23:06,560
higher than whatever Trefi offers for two reasons. One is that the composability aspect of crypto

227
00:23:06,560 --> 00:23:12,560
assets that makes it a lot more appealing from an APY standpoint. This was something that we

228
00:23:12,560 --> 00:23:18,080
took advantage of when we launched ETH market back in 2023. We were basically stacking up the

229
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:24,880
yield of Rocket, Aura and Balancer. That would be a lot harder to achieve outside of DeFi context.

230
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:32,240
With that, very important offerings. I do think that the yield in crypto should be higher,

231
00:23:32,240 --> 00:23:40,000
naturally. Secondly, crypto like DeFi is generally more efficient. I think protocols across the board

232
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:46,480
don't need to have a lot of headcount to monitor transactions. That should help with the margin.

233
00:23:47,840 --> 00:23:53,760
Without incurring a lot of overhead, the cost to the protocol operator also lowers.

234
00:23:54,480 --> 00:23:59,120
All things considered, assuming status quo on the yield between the DeFi and DeFi products,

235
00:23:59,120 --> 00:24:05,360
I think DeFi, because of the lower overhead and the cost is lower, the effective return

236
00:24:05,360 --> 00:24:09,760
would be more attractive for participants. Yeah, and you have natural capital efficiency

237
00:24:09,760 --> 00:24:14,320
because if you have a staked ETH, you can then take that staked ETH where you're already earning

238
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:19,360
the yield for staking it and earn rewards for depositing it into a two-sided pool.

239
00:24:19,360 --> 00:24:22,080
You're getting the trading and staking yield. Correct.

240
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:27,120
What other protocols are you interested in? What assets do you think we'll see emerge

241
00:24:27,120 --> 00:24:30,720
in the near future that you guys would work with or see as being high potential?

242
00:24:31,520 --> 00:24:34,560
I maintain the view that yield on Bitcoin is

243
00:24:35,360 --> 00:24:42,480
quite inevitable, but right now we don't have a very good way to generate yield on Bitcoin.

244
00:24:42,480 --> 00:24:46,080
Is that the number one desired thing for users in your view?

245
00:24:46,080 --> 00:24:50,400
I'm not sure about the broader user base, but at least for me, that would be the case.

246
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:54,960
Earning yield on stables is attractive and I think it will continue to exist.

247
00:24:54,960 --> 00:25:00,560
But just given the sheer size of Bitcoin right now, to be able to generate, I don't know,

248
00:25:00,560 --> 00:25:05,840
like two or 3% of Bitcoin, that would be pretty sweet. I'm sure there are multiple sources of

249
00:25:05,840 --> 00:25:13,120
yield on Bitcoin right now, but they might not be on-chain. So lending seems to me to be the one

250
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:20,960
that is most consistent and broad-based. And then we also have structured products. Some of the

251
00:25:20,960 --> 00:25:25,840
OTC desk, they could be structuring products that offer yield on Bitcoin. But beyond that,

252
00:25:25,840 --> 00:25:29,440
I'm not seeing too many opportunities. And then if you look at money markets,

253
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:36,080
red BTC on Aave, the APY is generally very low. I think that is definitely an avenue that

254
00:25:36,080 --> 00:25:40,560
would be interesting. But in the near term, I'm not too sure what needs to take place

255
00:25:40,560 --> 00:25:44,080
before we see greater adoption in that particular segment.

256
00:25:44,080 --> 00:25:48,400
Yeah, we completely agree. It almost feels like an irony that we haven't created

257
00:25:48,400 --> 00:25:52,880
good yield around Bitcoin when the things that crypto has produced are Bitcoin and yield.

258
00:25:52,880 --> 00:25:57,920
Whereas if you want to go borrow Bitcoin outside of DeFi through CIFI off-chain,

259
00:25:57,920 --> 00:26:01,680
it's typically double digit percent. So it shows you that the yield is there.

260
00:26:01,680 --> 00:26:06,160
It just hasn't been created in DeFi yet. I'm curious, why do people borrow Bitcoin?

261
00:26:06,160 --> 00:26:10,960
Is it too short? That's a good question. I don't know why people borrow Bitcoin. I think there are

262
00:26:10,960 --> 00:26:15,920
a number of reasons. Potentially to have more exposure to Bitcoin, right? Yeah, I'm curious.

263
00:26:15,920 --> 00:26:18,560
You use your Bitcoin collateral and borrow more Bitcoin?

264
00:26:18,560 --> 00:26:21,680
No, I don't. I use it as a sort of value, personally.

265
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:25,200
Right. I'm saying some people could though. Yeah, some people do that, yes.

266
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:29,200
Yeah, I think it's just people who want to have as much exposure to Bitcoin as they possibly can.

267
00:26:30,400 --> 00:26:32,240
Okay, okay. Fair enough.

268
00:26:32,240 --> 00:26:34,480
But yeah, the rates are in the 10 plus percent.

269
00:26:34,480 --> 00:26:35,440
I'm told, yes.

270
00:26:36,080 --> 00:26:40,000
What are the wrapped BTC rates on Aave actually that now that you mentioned? I haven't checked.

271
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:45,280
It's definitely less than 1%. Yeah, I'm not too sure at this point. Big difference, yeah?

272
00:26:45,280 --> 00:26:49,520
Yeah, no kidding. That's quite a spread. We clearly haven't gotten to efficiency quite yet.

273
00:26:50,240 --> 00:26:54,000
On that point about Bitcoin, I also wanted to ask you, how do you view the different

274
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:58,000
blockchains in making business decisions? Because there's obviously a core difference

275
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:01,600
when we talk about native Bitcoin here versus Bitcoin existing on other chains.

276
00:27:01,600 --> 00:27:04,560
And how do you guys select for chains and assets?

277
00:27:04,560 --> 00:27:09,120
Yeah, so for us, it's fairly straightforward because we are a second order derivative.

278
00:27:09,120 --> 00:27:14,320
So that means without the primitives and then without the TBL, it really wouldn't make sense

279
00:27:14,320 --> 00:27:19,200
for Pendle to exist. We need deal bearing assets before Pendle would make sense.

280
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:25,200
So when we evaluate whether to go onto a new chain or a new ecosystem,

281
00:27:25,200 --> 00:27:30,320
we would typically just observe whether there are good assets for Pendle to work with.

282
00:27:30,320 --> 00:27:33,680
And then if they are, how's the TBL traction?

283
00:27:34,320 --> 00:27:39,120
If the TBL traction is still very low, that means it might be premature.

284
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:41,120
And then we'll give it some time to observe.

285
00:27:41,200 --> 00:27:46,400
And if it grows to, let's say, a couple of hundred million dollars in TBL size,

286
00:27:46,400 --> 00:27:49,120
then it might make sense for Pendle to deploy there.

287
00:27:49,120 --> 00:27:55,600
Because from our perspective, whether it's five mil in TBL or $500 million in TBL,

288
00:27:55,600 --> 00:27:59,840
the effort to deploy is consistent. We still need to work with Oracle.

289
00:27:59,840 --> 00:28:03,280
We still need to maintain an infrastructure to run the products.

290
00:28:03,920 --> 00:28:08,800
So it would make sense for us to prioritize the one with higher TBL,

291
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:11,520
just because the return would be more meaningful.

292
00:28:11,520 --> 00:28:16,480
Sure. Makes sense. So you guys wait until you tell that there's something meaningful

293
00:28:16,480 --> 00:28:20,880
in an ecosystem and then you go versus trying to go to the new chain pre-launch

294
00:28:20,880 --> 00:28:22,160
and know that it's going to work.

295
00:28:22,960 --> 00:28:26,000
That's right. There's no first-world advantage for Pendle.

296
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,880
You guys have emerged and done incredible things in the space.

297
00:28:28,880 --> 00:28:33,120
I think a lot of people in the DeFi sphere would say to the point that people in conversation

298
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:36,800
talk about Uniswap, talk about Athena, talk about Aerodrome, talk about Pendle.

299
00:28:37,440 --> 00:28:44,080
Do you think DeFi activity will center around a number of these really meaningful applications

300
00:28:44,080 --> 00:28:46,080
or do you think there will be a longer tail?

301
00:28:46,640 --> 00:28:48,560
How do you think activity spreads out eventually?

302
00:28:49,200 --> 00:28:53,360
I think activities will likely concentrate around a few core protocols.

303
00:28:54,160 --> 00:28:58,480
I think people generally gravitate toward familiarity.

304
00:28:58,480 --> 00:29:01,920
And especially in DeFi, with new products, there are always

305
00:29:02,560 --> 00:29:07,840
risks that you might not be able to see, especially if it's very fresh in the market.

306
00:29:08,880 --> 00:29:14,000
For example, like Athena, it's been around and Uniswap, it's been around for years now.

307
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:16,720
The trust level for these products is very high.

308
00:29:16,720 --> 00:29:21,600
And the fact that these products have processed many, many billions of dollars

309
00:29:22,160 --> 00:29:23,680
is definitely very valuable.

310
00:29:24,560 --> 00:29:30,160
Relative to a product that has only been around for two days and processed maybe

311
00:29:30,160 --> 00:29:35,360
$100 in trading volume, as a user, you would naturally feel more comfortable with a product

312
00:29:35,360 --> 00:29:37,040
that is a lot more established.

313
00:29:37,040 --> 00:29:39,120
And I think that wouldn't change with time.

314
00:29:39,120 --> 00:29:43,280
And you guys are doing an assessment of how trusting your users will be of those partners,

315
00:29:43,280 --> 00:29:46,240
right? Because when they use Pendle in an Athena offering,

316
00:29:46,240 --> 00:29:48,640
it's two degrees of trust they're implementing.

317
00:29:48,640 --> 00:29:53,360
Yes, which is why I think it's important for us to know who we're dealing with.

318
00:29:53,360 --> 00:29:56,240
We are more open now from an infrastructure standpoint,

319
00:29:56,880 --> 00:30:04,080
but even then, we still need to do our best to do a basic due diligence on the counterparts.

320
00:30:04,880 --> 00:30:08,800
Do you expect you'll one day have tokenized equities on Pendle,

321
00:30:08,800 --> 00:30:10,400
earning yield in DeFi somehow?

322
00:30:10,400 --> 00:30:13,200
Potentially, yes. I would like that, yeah.

323
00:30:13,200 --> 00:30:16,320
Is that in the near future? How long do you think it takes us to get there?

324
00:30:16,320 --> 00:30:21,600
I think it ultimately depends on the protocol that issues equities.

325
00:30:21,600 --> 00:30:24,320
Yeah, so if they tokenize equities on chain,

326
00:30:24,320 --> 00:30:26,320
that is potentially something that we can work with.

327
00:30:26,880 --> 00:30:30,880
And then the yield accrued, the YT would be maybe dividends.

328
00:30:31,440 --> 00:30:34,400
That's interesting. I mean, I hope you can also,

329
00:30:34,400 --> 00:30:38,880
when we have tokenized equities in DeFi, earn yield on them as you would with DeFi assets,

330
00:30:38,880 --> 00:30:42,000
just by providing liquidity to a pool or something along those lines.

331
00:30:42,800 --> 00:30:44,080
Yeah, we'll see, perhaps.

332
00:30:44,720 --> 00:30:48,560
I wanted to ask you in closing, is there anything that keeps you up at night,

333
00:30:48,560 --> 00:30:52,960
or anything that you worry about when it comes to the ecosystem currently or Pendle?

334
00:30:53,520 --> 00:30:58,000
I want to say that as we grow and as we scale up,

335
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:01,040
because we have also increased the surface area that we're operating,

336
00:31:01,840 --> 00:31:04,800
there are a lot more problems that we are encountering.

337
00:31:05,520 --> 00:31:10,960
But in a way that I can really appreciate, because I think when we were smaller,

338
00:31:10,960 --> 00:31:14,800
we had maybe problems, but they were a lot more miniscule.

339
00:31:14,800 --> 00:31:18,560
Right now, we're operating a much bigger protocol.

340
00:31:18,560 --> 00:31:22,640
And then we also have a lot more problems compared to before.

341
00:31:22,720 --> 00:31:26,640
But I think this is a good sign that we have grown.

342
00:31:26,640 --> 00:31:32,800
And fortunately for me, by now, we have a very, very capable team of operators.

343
00:31:33,520 --> 00:31:38,480
So each and every one of us, we take care of our own vertical.

344
00:31:38,480 --> 00:31:42,640
And that allows me to sleep more peacefully at night.

345
00:31:43,360 --> 00:31:46,560
But that aside, there are definitely a lot of considerations.

346
00:31:46,560 --> 00:31:48,240
How do we continue to scale up?

347
00:31:48,240 --> 00:31:53,520
Because ultimately, I'd like for Pendle to not exist only for two years.

348
00:31:53,520 --> 00:31:56,720
I'd like for Pendle to continue to be relevant for a long, long time,

349
00:31:57,280 --> 00:32:01,120
and grow into new segments, take on new challenges,

350
00:32:01,120 --> 00:32:03,920
and be world-class in our product offerings.

351
00:32:04,480 --> 00:32:08,400
And then thinking about how the ecosystem could change,

352
00:32:08,400 --> 00:32:11,920
what kind of things that we should be anticipating and prepare.

353
00:32:11,920 --> 00:32:15,120
These are definitely something that I think about very much.

354
00:32:15,680 --> 00:32:17,840
That makes sense. And I'll leave it there.

